  {
   "id": "812tDeVzAwAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2019-05-15T00:12:22Z",
   "subject": "Re: How to choose model when AIC and BIC are opposite?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">These are information criteria, so &quot;better&quot; might not be the best description. According to BIC, which has a higher standard for sample size information and model parsimony, the first model is better. If sample size is less of a factor, and only model parsimony is the goal, then AIC suggests the second model. It depends on what you think is more informative in your empirical context.<div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tue, May 14, 2019 at 3:56 AM Dorothy Miller &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">778...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
